  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain and thunderstorms possible Thursday into\nFriday.\n\nCAM models still suggest some low level moisture convergence along \nthe southern Blue Ridge and into the Alleghany Highlands today,\nbut the overall likelihood for precipitation is low. Dew points\nhave recently crawled back into the lower 60s, but the \ninfluence of a surface high as well as lack of any synoptic lift\nwill put a cap on any serious thunderstorm development. \nThursday is a different story as moisture recovery is expected \nto be complete with dew points returning back to the 60s and 70s\nby tomorrow afternoon. With air temperatures also increasing to\nthe mid-80s to lower 90s, there will be enough instability for \nthunderstorm development. Convection will also be assisted by \nvorticity that gets sent to our region from a shortwave that \ndevelops to our southwest in Tennessee. This wave is projected \nto intersect the TN/NC border but southwesterly winds will \nlikely send some of its vorticity maxima to our region. \nEffective wind shear of 25-35 knots coupled with SBCAPE values \nup to 2500 J/kg will support the production and organization of \ncluster thunderstorms with large hail and wet microbursts. The \nStorm Prediction Center has issued a Marginal Risk of Severe \nWeather for Thursday due to these hazards. \n\nA flood threat is possible as well given PWATs also rebuild back up \nto 1.75\" by tomorrow. Repeated rounds of thunderstorms increase\nthe risk of flash flooding in vulnerable and localized areas. A\ncomponent of the flood concern, however, is due to a cold front\nthat is projected to arrive shortly after Thursday's storms. \nModels have struggled to narrow down the timing of this system \nbut it is currently forecast to arrive between late Thursday \nnight into early Friday morning. Confidence is low it will \narrive early enough to affect Thursday afternoon's convection. \nNevertheless, it may trail getting over here and keep \nprecipitation chances going well into Friday morning. For now, \nthe Weather Prediction Center has issued a Marginal Risk of \nExcessive Rainfall for Thursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Hot conditions return early next week.\n\nFollowing the storms, a mid-level ridge over the southwestern United \nStates will migrate towards the southeastern United States. The \ncenter of this air mass will likely be just north enough to prevent \nany ridge riders from bringing storms to our area. The increase\nin 500mb heights will result in warmer temperatures with highs \nin the mid-80s to upper 90s. The heat indices may reach into the\nlower 100s in the Piedmont by early next week. This forecast \nperiod is still several days out but will be monitored for any \npotential heat risks to the public.",
